\label{fig:nve_trajectory}
\end{figure}

The trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO in all three tested seeds (peak NVE: $4.12\times$, $1.62\times$, $1.53\times$; all $\geq 1.5\times$ threshold), universally degrading performance to below 30\% of baseline (mean eval 206.2, 22.5\% of baseline). Downstream trajectories diverge: seed~1 (008d) undergoes immediate full collapse (eval 82.8, 9.0\% of baseline), seed~3 (008d\_s3) exhibits late-onset collapse still in progress at 105K (eval 268, train returns declining 70\% over final 8K steps), while seed~2 (009b) self-corrects after transient overestimation (eval 267.9, 29.3\% of baseline). This pattern establishes universal CIVO onset with stochastic downstream resolution: collapse is the majority outcome (2/3 seeds) but exhibits variable onset timing (35K vs 70K steps). The 1/3 self-correction case remains severely degraded. This confirms that CIVO universally damages performance regardless of whether full collapse follows (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

%% ============================================================
%% F3--F5: Summary (details in Appendix B)
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly necessary} for CIVO. Neither factor alone triggers pathological overestimation: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, NVE $= 0.93\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, NVE $= 0.97\times$) both remain well below the $1.5\times$ diagnostic threshold, while frozen embedding under full exposure (015b) peaks near the diagnostic threshold ($1.45\times$ at 55K) but retreats to $0.55\times$ by 105K; frozen embedding thus prevents the sustained escalation characteristic of CIVO (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial_grid}). Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}).

%% ============================================================
%% F6--F7: Interventions and cross-environment
%% ============================================================

\subsection{F6--F7: Interventions and Multiple Pathways}
\label{sec:f6}

\paragraph{F6: $\dt{=}1$ imagination does not prevent all collapse.}
Exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding. Seed~1 is healthy (eval 869.9, $\nve = 0.94\times$); seed~2 collapses (eval $715 \rightarrow 137$, $\nve = 3.78\times$), but via a qualitatively distinct mechanism (Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}). Classic CIVO (008d, Pathway~A) shows value explosion followed by evaluation decline with decreasing entropy; 008e\_v3\_s2 (Pathway~B) shows evaluation crash with \emph{increasing} entropy ($-5.21 \rightarrow -3.83$) while values remain stable. This produces the sole false positive in the NVE classification.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}
\caption{Contrasting collapse dynamics. \textbf{Left:} 008d (CIVO), value explosion with entropy decrease. \textbf{Right:} 008e\_v3 s2 (non-CIVO), evaluation crash with entropy increase.}
\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{F7: Full-pathway gradient stopping shows mixed results.}
Exp\_018 applies Selective Gradient Stopping (SGS) by detaching all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$; this blocks the observation decoder pathway identified in F2 as the primary CIVO conduit. Figure~\ref{fig:civo_decomposition} decomposes evaluation returns across conditions. Trained $\dt$-embedding alone produces $-73\%$ peak and $-86\%$ final eval degradation relative to the baseline. Full-SGS seed~1 avoids collapse (eval \sgseval{}, $-51\%$ final relative to baseline) while seed~2 collapses (eval 40.8, $-96\%$); the overall recovery rate is 50\%. SGS reduces but does not eliminate CIVO risk: the gradient pathway it blocks is necessary but not sufficient for reliable elimination. Reward-only SGS (016e) fails entirely (NVE $10.42\times$). Cross-environment validation on Cheetah Run (010b) confirms CIVO generalization: evaluation peaks at 321.9 before declining to 51.7 at 105K ($-84\%$, minimum 22.7 at 65K); peak NVE $8.27\times$. Full-SGS on Cheetah Run enables recovery from transient CIVO onset (NVE $9.94\times$ at 45K, eval 4.6) to functional performance (eval 221.5 at 105K), in contrast to permanent collapse without SGS (010b: eval 51.7). Combined with Walker results (50\% recovery rate), SGS is necessary but not sufficient for CIVO elimination.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_civo_decomposition.pdf}
\caption{CIVO decomposition: peak and final (105K step) evaluation returns on Walker Walk (DMC) across four conditions. \textbf{Baseline} (001): vanilla DreamerV3, no temporal embedding. \textbf{Trained dt-emb} (008d)\footnotemark: DreamerV3 with a learned $d{=}32$ temporal embedding exposed to multi-rate data. \textbf{Full-SGS} (s1, s2): trained embedding with Selective Gradient Stopping that detaches all non-KL gradients from $e_{\dt}$, two seeds. Trained $\dt$-embedding degrades peak eval by $73\%$ and final eval by $86\%$ relative to the baseline. Full-SGS seed~1 partially recovers ($-51\%$ final, no collapse) but seed~2 collapses ($-96\%$ final, marked ``collapsed''), indicating that SGS enables recovery from CIVO in some seeds but is not sufficient for reliable prevention (recovery rate $= 50\%$). All percentages relative to Baseline 001.}
